# stackguardian_policy (Resource)

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

## Scoping a policy

`enforced_on` decides where the policy applies: `["*"]` for the whole organization, or any
combination of workflow groups, workflows and connectors — a workflow group is `/wfgrps/<group>`,
with no trailing slash.

Note this is **not** the same form a `stackguardian_role` uses in `allowed_permissions`, which
takes bare resource names. See
[Resource IDs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/StackGuardian/stackguardian/latest/docs/guides/ResourceIDs).

Scope and approval settings apply only to `policy_type = "GENERAL"`. A `FILTER.INSIGHT` policy
filters Insight findings rather than gating a run, so it takes neither.

## Where the policy body comes from

`policy_vcs_config` says where the body comes from. An inline policy sets it *and*
`policy_input_data`, which carries the body itself — the two are not alternatives.

- **Written inline** — set `policy_input_data`, and point `custom_source` at
  `SG_POLICY_FRAMEWORK` / `INLINE`.
- **From the marketplace** — set `use_marketplace_template = true` and `policy_template_id`.
  No `policy_input_data`: the template carries the body.
- **From your own repository** — set `custom_source` to the policy kind (`OPA_REGO`,
  `SG_POLICY_FRAMEWORK`) and point its `config` at the repository.

Omitting `policy_vcs_config` is valid: the backend applies the inline configuration at
runtime, and a policy read back from the platform always carries it. The provider does not
fill it in for you, so writing it out explicitly is what keeps configuration and state
describing the same thing.

